@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Octo 2: arrays and indexing)

Everything in Octo is an array. These blocks show how to build, reshape and index them.

@section(Building arrays)

Brackets build by hand, the colon builds ranges, and the constructor functions build the common shapes.

@octo
r = [1 2 3], c = [1; 2; 3]
t = 0:0.25:1
linspace(0, 1, 5)
logspace(0, 2, 3)
zeros(2, 3), ones(2), eye(3)
rand(2) < 2
randi(6, 1, 4) > 0
size(randn(3, 2))
repmat([1 2], 2, 2)
magic(4)
diag([1 2 3])
@end

@section(Size and shape)

`size`, `numel` and `reshape` describe and change shape; `(:)` always gives a column.

@octo
A = reshape(1:6, 2, 3)
size(A), numel(A), rows(A), columns(A), ndims(A), length(A)
A(:)'
A'
isempty([]), isvector(1:3), isscalar(5), ismatrix(A), isrow(1:3), iscolumn((1:3)')
squeeze(ones(1, 1, 3))'
permute(reshape(1:8, 2, 2, 2), [2 1 3])(:, :, 1)
@end

@section(Indexing)

Index by position, by `end`, by logical mask, or by linear index.

@octo
A = magic(4);
A(2, 3), A(end, end), A(2, :), A(:, 1)'
A(A > 12)'
[r, c] = find(A == 16)
idx = sub2ind(size(A), 2, 3), [i, j] = ind2sub(size(A), 10)
B = A; B(1, :) = []; size(B)
B(B > 10) = 0
@end

@section(Joining, sorting and searching)

These are the everyday array tools.

@octo
x = [4 1 3 1 5];
cat(1, x, x), horzcat(x, 9), vertcat(x, x)
[s, k] = sort(x, "descend")
sortrows([3 1; 1 2; 2 0])
unique(x), fliplr(x), flipud([1; 2]), circshift(x, 1)
rot90([1 2; 3 4]), numel(randperm(5))
[m, im] = max(x), min(x), cumsum(x), cumprod([1 2 3]), diff(x)
sum(magic(3)), sum(magic(3), 2)', prod([1 2 3 4]), mean(x)
nnz(x > 2), numel(find(x == 1))
@end

@section(Sets)

Set functions treat arrays as sets and return sorted results.

@octo
a = [1 2 3 4]; b = [3 4 5];
union(a, b), intersect(a, b), setdiff(a, b), setxor(a, b)
ismember(2, a), [tf, loc] = ismember([5 3], a)
@end

@section(Vectorised arithmetic)

Dotted operators work element by element; broadcasting stretches a row against a column.

@octo
a = [1 2 3];
a .* a, a ./ 2, a .^ 2
a' + a
bsxfun(@plus, a', a)
@end
